-- title:
--   Module-finite local algebras over henselian local rings are henselian
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is a henselian local ring, that is, a local ring in which for every monic $f \in R[X]$ and every $a \in R$ with $f(a)$ in the maximal ideal and $f'(a)$ a unit there is a root $b$ of $f$ in $R$ congruent to $a$ modulo the maximal ideal. Let $S$ be a commutative ring which is local (its non-units form an ideal) and which is an $R$-algebra that is finite as an $R$-module. The conclusion is that $S$ is itself a henselian local ring: for every monic $f \in S[X]$ and every $a \in S$ with $f(a)$ in the maximal ideal of $S$ and $f'(a)$ a unit in $S$, there exists $b \in S$ with $f(b) = 0$ and $b - a$ in the maximal ideal of $S$. No separability, flatness or finiteness hypothesis on $R$ beyond henselianness is required, and $S$ is not assumed to be an integral domain. The proof uses the decomposition of an arbitrary module-finite algebra over a henselian local ring into a finite product of local factors, in the form of a complete family of orthogonal idempotents $e_{\mathfrak m}$ indexed by the (finite) maximal spectrum of the algebra, with $e_{\mathfrak m} \notin \mathfrak m$, $e_{\mathfrak m} \in \mathfrak m'$ for $\mathfrak m' \neq \mathfrak m$, and each quotient by the ideal generated by $1 - e_{\mathfrak m}$ local.
--
--   This is the standard permanence property of henselian local rings: henselianness passes to module-finite local algebras. It is the base case for the analogous statement for integral local algebras, [`HenselianLocalRing.of_isIntegral_of_isLocalRing`](thm.html#HenselianLocalRing.of_isIntegral_of_isLocalRing), and is used in the construction of finite étale neighbourhoods of smooth morphisms over henselian local base rings and in base-change arguments for regular local rings.
